      "In an era dominated by online bootcamps, executive summary apps, and promises of 'mastering coding in twelve weeks' or 'becoming an investor over a weekend,' society has forgotten the profound distinction between explicit information and tacit knowledge. Explicit information is propositional: it can be codified into textbooks, summarized in bulleted lists, and transferred through video lectures. You can memorize the rules of chess in twenty minutes; you can read the complete syntax manual of a programming language in an afternoon.",
      "Tacit knowledge, by contrast, is somatic, contextual, and deeply experiential. It is the intuitive grasp of how the wood grain will behave under a specific chisel bevel; the instantaneous clinical instinct that tells an emergency physician a patient is deteriorating before vital signs drop; the subtle architectural judgment that warns a senior engineer that a distributed microservice topology will encounter deadlock under high concurrent loads. Tacit knowledge cannot be downloaded, summarized, or compressed; it can only be absorbed through thousands of hours of direct friction against reality under the guidance of seasoned tradition.",
